A Wearable Plasma-Based Oxygen Generator for Martian Extravehicular Activity: System Architecture, Mathematical Foundations, and Control Design

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

Date: April 2026

---

Abstract

We present the complete system architecture, engineering specifications, and mathematical foundations for a wearable plasma-based oxygen generator designed to augment extravehicular activity (EVA) life support on Mars. The device processes ambient Martian atmosphere (600 Pa, 95% CO₂, 210 K) to produce 10–20 g O₂ per hour—sufficient to offset metabolic oxygen consumption during EVA—while maintaining a compact, low-mass form factor (<5 kg, <400 W). The system integrates a two-stage electrostatic precipitator and inertial cyclone for dust filtration, a diffuse glow discharge plasma reactor for CO₂ dissociation, a catalytic post-processor for CO oxidation and O₃ decomposition, and a comprehensive sensor suite for real-time state estimation. We model the coupled plasma–dust–rarefied gas dynamics as an age-augmented piecewise-deterministic Markov process (PDMP) and prove that the re-architected system restores structural identifiability and observability—properties that are lost in conventional filamentary DBD designs due to stochastic dust–plasma coupling. A reduced-order model based on proper orthogonal decomposition (POD) enables real-time implementation of an Interacting Multiple Model (IMM) particle filter for hybrid state estimation. A safety-critical model predictive controller (MPC) derived from the extended generator of the PDMP guarantees avoidance of unsafe regimes (plasma quench, overheating, catalyst deactivation) while maintaining oxygen production within prescribed bounds. This work provides the theoretical blueprint for a predictable, certifiable wearable plasma ISRU device suitable for human Mars missions.

---

1. Introduction

1.1 Motivation

Sustained human presence on Mars requires in-situ resource utilization (ISRU) to reduce reliance on Earth-supplied consumables. The Martian atmosphere—composed of approximately 95% CO₂ at a surface pressure of 600 Pa and temperatures averaging 210 K—offers an abundant feedstock for oxygen production. NASA's MOXIE experiment aboard the Perseverance rover has successfully demonstrated solid-oxide electrolysis of CO₂ at a rate of 6–8 g O₂ per hour, validating the fundamental feasibility of ISRU oxygen generation [1].

For extravehicular activity (EVA), however, the requirements differ substantially from surface-deployed ISRU. An EVA suit must provide life support for 6–8 hours while minimizing mass and volume. Current portable life support systems (PLSS) rely on stored oxygen and CO₂ scrubbing; augmenting or replacing stored O₂ with in situ generation could dramatically extend EVA duration or reduce suit mass. A wearable plasma-based oxygen generator—operating directly from ambient Martian atmosphere—offers a compelling pathway to this goal.

1.2 Challenges of Wearable Plasma ISRU

Miniaturizing a plasma reactor to a wearable form factor forces the system into a coupled physical regime where conventional modeling assumptions fail:

· Rarefied gas transport. At 600 Pa, the mean free path of CO₂ is approximately 50 μm. For a reactor gap of 3 mm and intake manifold diameter of 1 cm, the Knudsen number $Kn \sim 10^{-2}$, placing the flow in the slip-flow regime where continuum Navier–Stokes equations require slip boundary corrections.
· Stochastic plasma dynamics. Dielectric barrier discharges (DBDs) at low pressure tend to operate in a filamentary mode characterized by random micro-discharge events. The spatial and temporal distribution of these filaments depends sensitively on surface charge accumulation, gas flow, and dust contamination.
· Electrostatically active Martian dust. Dust particles entrained in the Martian atmosphere acquire charge through triboelectric and plasma interactions, distort local electric fields, and deposit on surfaces, altering both plasma characteristics and flow boundary conditions.

Prior work [2] demonstrated that when these couplings are modeled as a piecewise-deterministic Markov process (PDMP), the resulting system is structurally non-identifiable: the vector of closure parameters (surface recombination probabilities, dust-induced field distortion, vibrational excitation efficiencies, catalyst activity) cannot be uniquely determined from any finite set of sensor measurements. Furthermore, the discrete regime state (flow, plasma, dust, catalyst modes) is unobservable under practical sensor configurations. This renders conventional DBD-based wearable ISRU unpredictable and uncertifiable as a life-support device.

1.3 Contribution

This paper presents a re-architected wearable plasma oxygen generator that restores identifiability and observability while meeting the mass, power, and safety requirements of Martian EVA. The key design innovations are:

1. Deterministic dust filtration. A two-stage electrostatic precipitator (ESP) and inertial cyclone remove >99% of Martian dust particles before they reach the plasma reactor, collapsing stochastic dust dynamics into a quasi-deterministic input.
2. Stable plasma regime. The filamentary DBD is replaced by a diffuse glow discharge operated under conditions that eliminate micro-discharge stochasticity. This is achieved through careful control of frequency, power, and gas composition.
3. Full-state observation and control architecture. A multi-modal sensor suite, combined with reduced-order modeling (POD) and an Interacting Multiple Model (IMM) particle filter, provides real-time estimates of the hybrid state. A model predictive controller (MPC) based on the extended generator of the PDMP guarantees avoidance of unsafe regimes.

We provide a complete mathematical construction of the system as an age-augmented PDMP, prove that identifiability and observability are restored, specify all subsystems with engineering parameters, and present the control algorithms in full detail. The result is a theoretical blueprint for a predictable, certifiable wearable plasma ISRU device.

1.4 Organization

Section 2 defines system requirements and presents the overall architecture. Section 3 details each subsystem with engineering specifications. Section 4 develops the mathematical model, including the PDMP formulation and reduced-order modeling. Section 5 proves identifiability and observability restoration. Section 6 describes the state estimation and control architecture. Section 7 summarizes predicted performance metrics. Section 8 concludes.

