2.3 Control Volume and Mass Flow

The oxygen production rate $\dot{m}_{O_2}$ is related to the intake mass flow $\dot{m}_{\text{in}}$ and the effective conversion efficiency $\eta$ by:

\dot{m}_{O_2} = \dot{m}_{\text{in}} \cdot Y_{CO_2}^{\text{atm}} \cdot \eta \cdot \frac{M_{O_2}}{M_{CO_2}}

where $Y_{CO_2}^{\text{atm}} \approx 0.95$, $M_{O_2} = 32$ g/mol, $M_{CO_2} = 44$ g/mol. To produce $\dot{m}_{O_2} = 15$ g/h at $\eta = 0.30$, the required intake mass flow is:

\dot{m}_{\text{in}} = \frac{15 \text{ g/h}}{0.95 \cdot 0.30 \cdot (32/44)} \approx 72 \text{ g/h} \approx 20 \text{ mg/s}.

At 600 Pa and 210 K, the atmospheric density is $\rho = p/(R_{\text{CO}_2} T) \approx 0.015 \text{ kg/m}^3$. The volumetric flow rate is $\dot{V} \approx 1.3 \text{ L/s}$, achievable with a small passive intake (cross‑sectional area ~10 cm²) under nominal wind or motion‑induced flow.

