3. Subsystem Specifications

3.1 Intake and Dust Filtration

Problem. Martian dust (JSC‑Mars‑1 simulant) exhibits bipolar, size‑dependent charging in low‑pressure CO₂ [3]. In a conventional DBD, dust particles distort the local electric field, trigger arcs, and deposit on dielectric surfaces, altering slip boundary conditions and surface recombination probabilities. This introduces stochastic, age‑dependent transition rates that collapse identifiability.

Solution. A two‑stage filtration system removes >99% of dust particles before the gas enters the plasma reactor.

Stage 1: Inertial Cyclone. A passive cyclone separator removes particles >5 μm via centrifugal force. Design parameters:

· Body diameter: 20 mm
· Inlet velocity: 5–15 m/s (induced by suit motion or ambient wind)
· Cut diameter $d_{50} \approx 2$ μm at Martian pressure [4]
· Pressure drop: <10 Pa

Stage 2: Electrostatic Precipitator (ESP). A wire‑cylinder ESP charges and collects submicron particles. Design parameters [5]:

· Cylinder diameter: 30 mm, length: 50 mm
· Corona wire: 0.1 mm diameter, applied voltage 2–3 kV DC
· Collection efficiency: >99% for 0.1–5 μm particles at 600 Pa CO₂
· Power consumption: <5 W
· Self‑cleaning via periodic polarity reversal (every 10–30 min)

