3.2 Plasma Reactor

Problem. Filamentary DBDs exhibit random micro‑discharge locations and timing, producing a stochastic point‑process of dissociation events. The local field approximation breaks down, and recombination in the afterglow limits conversion efficiency [6].

Solution. We adopt a diffuse glow discharge operating in the α‑mode of a DBD or a plasma‑membrane hybrid. Under appropriate conditions (pressure × gap ~1–10 Torr·cm, frequency 15–25 kHz, moderate power density), the discharge fills the gap uniformly [7].

Reactor Geometry:

· Coaxial configuration: inner electrode radius $r_i = 3$ mm, dielectric tube inner radius $r_d = 6$ mm
· Gap: $d = 3$ mm
· Length: $L = 100$ mm (active volume ~8.5 cm³)
· Dielectric material: Al₂O₃ (εᵣ ≈ 9), thickness 1 mm
· Electrodes: stainless steel

