3.3 Post‑Processing and Oxygen Separation

Problem. The plasma effluent contains CO, O, O₃, and residual CO₂. CO is toxic; O₃ is a respiratory irritant. O atoms recombine on surfaces, but this must be controlled to maximize O₂ yield.

Solution. A two‑stage post‑processor:

Stage 1: Catalytic CO Oxidation. A platinum‑on‑alumina catalyst promotes the reaction $\text{CO} + \frac{1}{2}\text{O}_2 \to \text{CO}_2$ at moderate temperatures (400–500 K). The exothermic reaction provides self‑heating.

· Catalyst mass: 100 g
· Operating temperature: 450 K (maintained by reaction heat + heater)
· Conversion: >99% CO removal

Stage 2: O₃ Decomposition. A manganese‑oxide catalyst decomposes residual O₃: $2\text{O}_3 \to 3\text{O}_2$.

· Catalyst mass: 50 g
· Operating temperature: 300–350 K
· Conversion: >99% O₃ removal

Oxygen Enrichment. The product stream is ~30–50% O₂, balance CO₂ and inert gases. For EVA breathing, >99.5% O₂ is required. A small pressure‑swing adsorption (PSA) unit using zeolite 13X separates O₂ from CO₂ and N₂/Ar.

