A.2 Electron Boltzmann Equation and BOLSIG+ Implementation

A.2.1 Two-Term Approximation

In the two-term spherical harmonic expansion, the EEDF is written as:

f(\mathbf{v}) = f_0(v) + \frac{\mathbf{v}}{v} \cdot \mathbf{f}_1(v)

where $f_0(v)$ is the isotropic part and $\mathbf{f}_1(v)$ is the anisotropic perturbation. Substituting into the Boltzmann equation yields the classical two-term equation for $f_0(\epsilon)$:

\frac{\partial}{\partial \epsilon} \left( W f_0 - D \frac{\partial f_0}{\partial \epsilon} \right) = S(f_0)

where:

· $W = -\frac{2m_e}{M} \nu_m \epsilon^2 \sigma_m$ (energy loss rate due to elastic collisions)
· $D = \frac{e^2 E^2}{3 m_e \nu_m} \epsilon \sigma_m$ (energy diffusion due to electric field)
· $\nu_m = N \sigma_m \sqrt{2\epsilon/m_e}$ is the momentum transfer collision frequency
· $S(f_0)$ represents inelastic collision processes

The term $W f_0 - D \frac{\partial f_0}{\partial \epsilon}$ represents the electron flux in energy space.

A.2.2 Numerical Solution with BOLSIG+

BOLSIG+ [A7] solves the two-term Boltzmann equation using a finite-difference discretization on a logarithmic energy grid. The solution procedure is:

1. Discretization: Energy grid $\epsilon_i$, $i = 1,\dots,N_\epsilon$, typically 200–400 points from 0 to 100 eV.
2. Iteration: The equation is nonlinear because the transport coefficients depend on $f_0$. Start with an initial guess (e.g., Maxwellian), compute collision frequencies, solve the linear system for a new $f_0$, and iterate until convergence ($\|f_0^{(k+1)} - f_0^{(k)}\| < 10^{-6}$).
3. Normalization: After convergence, normalize such that $\int_0^\infty \epsilon^{1/2} f_0(\epsilon) d\epsilon = 1$.
4. Rate Coefficients: For each reaction $j$:
   k_j = \sqrt{\frac{2e}{m_e}} \int_0^\infty \epsilon \, \sigma_j(\epsilon) \, f_0(\epsilon) \, d\epsilon
5. Transport Coefficients:
   · Electron mobility: $\mu_e N = -\frac{1}{3} \sqrt{\frac{2e}{m_e}} \int_0^\infty \frac{\epsilon}{\sigma_m} \frac{\partial f_0}{\partial \epsilon} d\epsilon$
   · Electron diffusion: $D_e N = \frac{1}{3} \sqrt{\frac{2e}{m_e}} \int_0^\infty \frac{\epsilon}{\sigma_m} f_0 d\epsilon$

For the Martian atmosphere composition (95% CO₂, 2.7% N₂, 1.6% Ar, 0.13% O₂), the cross-sections are combined using mole-fraction weighting.

A.2.3 Reduced Electric Field Scaling

At 600 Pa and 210 K, the gas number density is:

N = \frac{p}{k_B T} = \frac{600}{1.38 \times 10^{-23} \times 210} \approx 2.07 \times 10^{23} \text{ m}^{-3} = 2.07 \times 10^{17} \text{ cm}^{-3}

The reduced electric field $E/N$ is expressed in Townsend (Td), where $1 \text{ Td} = 10^{-21} \text{ V·m}^2 = 10^{-17} \text{ V·cm}^2$. For an applied field $E = V_0/d = 10 \text{ kV} / 3 \text{ mm} \approx 3.3 \times 10^6 \text{ V/m}$, we have:

E/N = \frac{3.3 \times 10^6}{2.07 \times 10^{23}} \approx 1.6 \times 10^{-17} \text{ V·m}^2 = 160 \text{ Td}

This is within the typical range for efficient CO₂ dissociation.

A.3 ZDPlasKin Implementation

ZDPlasKin [A8] is a zero-dimensional plasma kinetics solver that tracks the temporal evolution of species densities. The governing ODE system is:

\frac{d n_i}{dt} = \sum_{j \in \text{reactions}} (\nu_{ij}^{(P)} - \nu_{ij}^{(R)}) \, k_j \prod_{l} n_l^{\nu_{lj}^{(R)}} + S_i - L_i

where:

· $n_i$ is the number density of species $i$
· $\nu_{ij}^{(P)}$, $\nu_{ij}^{(R)}$ are stoichiometric coefficients (products and reactants)
· $k_j$ is the rate coefficient for reaction $j$
· $S_i$, $L_i$ are source and loss terms (e.g., wall losses)

The gas temperature evolution includes heating from:

· Elastic electron-neutral collisions: $P_{\text{elas}} = \frac{3 m_e}{M} n_e \nu_m k_B (T_e - T_g)$
· Vibrational-translational relaxation: $P_{VT} = \sum_v \frac{\epsilon_v}{\tau_{VT}} (n_v - n_v^{\text{eq}})$
· Exothermic neutral reactions: $P_{\text{chem}} = \sum_j \Delta H_j R_j$

The temperature equation is:

\frac{d T_g}{dt} = \frac{1}{C_V} \left( P_{\text{elas}} + P_{VT} + P_{\text{chem}} - \frac{T_g - T_w}{\tau_{\text{th}}} \right)

where $C_V = \sum_i n_i c_{v,i}$ is the volumetric heat capacity and $\tau_{\text{th}}$ is the thermal relaxation time to the wall.

