Appendix B: Rarefied Gas Dynamics and Slip-Flow Boundary Conditions

B.1 Knudsen Number Regimes

The Knudsen number $Kn = \lambda / L$ characterizes the degree of rarefaction. The mean free path for CO₂ at 600 Pa and 210 K is:

\lambda = \frac{k_B T}{\sqrt{2} \pi d^2 p} = \frac{1.38 \times 10^{-23} \times 210}{\sqrt{2} \pi (4.6 \times 10^{-10})^2 \times 600} \approx 5.2 \times 10^{-5} \text{ m} = 52 \text{ μm}

For characteristic lengths:

· DBD gap $L_{\text{gap}} = 3$ mm: $Kn = 0.017$ (slip-flow regime)
· Intake manifold $L_{\text{intake}} = 10$ mm: $Kn = 0.005$ (near-continuum)

Flow regimes:

· $Kn < 0.01$: Continuum (Navier–Stokes with no-slip)
· $0.01 < Kn < 0.1$: Slip-flow (Navier–Stokes with slip boundary conditions)
· $0.1 < Kn < 10$: Transition (DSMC or higher-order moment methods required)
· $Kn > 10$: Free molecular flow

Our system operates in the slip-flow regime, where the Navier–Stokes equations remain valid in the bulk but require modified boundary conditions.

B.2 Maxwell's First-Order Slip Boundary Condition

For isothermal flow, Maxwell derived the slip velocity:

u_{\text{slip}} = u_{\text{wall}} + \frac{2 - \sigma_v}{\sigma_v} \lambda \left( \frac{\partial u}{\partial n} \right)_{\text{wall}}

where:

· $u_{\text{wall}}$ is the wall velocity (zero for stationary walls)
· $\sigma_v \in [0,1]$ is the tangential momentum accommodation coefficient (TMAC)
· $\frac{\partial u}{\partial n}$ is the normal velocity gradient at the wall

For CO₂ on Al₂O₃ at 210 K, $\sigma_v$ is not well-characterized. Literature values for similar gas-surface pairs range from 0.7 to 1.0. We adopt $\sigma_v = 0.85$ as a nominal value.

The slip length is defined as:

\lambda_s = \frac{2 - \sigma_v}{\sigma_v} \lambda \approx 1.35 \lambda \approx 70 \text{ μm}

For velocity profile in a cylindrical tube of radius $R$, the slip-corrected Poiseuille flow is:

u(r) = \frac{1}{4\mu} \left( -\frac{dp}{dz} \right) \left[ R^2 - r^2 + 2 R \lambda_s \right]

The volumetric flow rate is enhanced by the factor $(1 + 4 \lambda_s / R)$ compared to the no-slip case.

B.3 Temperature Jump Boundary Condition

Analogous to velocity slip, a temperature jump occurs at the wall:

T_{\text{gas}} - T_{\text{wall}} = \frac{2 - \sigma_T}{\sigma_T} \frac{2\gamma}{\gamma+1} \frac{\lambda}{\Pr} \left( \frac{\partial T}{\partial n} \right)_{\text{wall}}

where:

· $\sigma_T$ is the thermal accommodation coefficient
· $\gamma = c_p/c_v$ is the specific heat ratio (1.29 for CO₂ at 210 K)
· $\Pr = \mu c_p / \kappa$ is the Prandtl number (0.75 for CO₂)

B.4 Species Slip and Diffusion Slip

For gas mixtures, additional slip phenomena arise. The diffusion slip velocity for species $i$ is:

u_{\text{diff},i} = \sum_{j \neq i} \frac{D_{ij}}{n} \frac{\partial n_j}{\partial x}

This is particularly relevant near the reactor walls where O and CO concentration gradients are steep due to surface recombination.

The combined slip boundary condition for species $i$ is:

u_i|_{\text{wall}} = u_{\text{wall}} + \lambda_s \frac{\partial u_i}{\partial n} + \sum_{j} \frac{D_{ij}}{n} \frac{\partial n_j}{\partial x}

