Appendix D: Proper Orthogonal Decomposition and Reduced-Order Modeling

D.1 Mathematical Formulation

Let the high-fidelity state vector $\mathbf{x}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^N$ (with $N \sim 10^5\text{--}10^6$) evolve according to:

\frac{d\mathbf{x}}{dt} = \mathbf{F}(\mathbf{x}, t; \boldsymbol{\mu})

where $\boldsymbol{\mu} \in \mathbb{R}^p$ are parameters. POD constructs an optimal low-dimensional subspace spanned by orthonormal basis vectors $\{\boldsymbol{\phi}_i\}_{i=1}^r$.

D.1.1 Snapshot Collection

Generate $N_s$ snapshots $\mathbf{x}_k = \mathbf{x}(t_k)$ at times $t_1, \dots, t_{N_s}$ from simulations covering the parameter space. Form the snapshot matrix:

\mathbf{X} = [\mathbf{x}_1, \mathbf{x}_2, \dots, \mathbf{x}_{N_s}] \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N_s}

Typically, the snapshot matrix is centered by subtracting the mean: $\tilde{\mathbf{X}} = \mathbf{X} - \bar{\mathbf{x}} \mathbf{1}^\top$.

D.1.2 Singular Value Decomposition

Compute the SVD:

\tilde{\mathbf{X}} = \mathbf{U} \boldsymbol{\Sigma} \mathbf{V}^\top

where:

· $\mathbf{U} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times N_s}$ has orthonormal columns (left singular vectors)
· $\boldsymbol{\Sigma} = \text{diag}(\sigma_1, \dots, \sigma_{N_s})$ with $\sigma_1 \ge \sigma_2 \ge \dots \ge 0$
· $\mathbf{V} \in \mathbb{R}^{N_s \times N_s}$ is orthogonal

The singular values $\sigma_i$ measure the energy captured by each mode.

D.1.3 Truncation

Select $r$ such that the relative energy retained exceeds a threshold:

\frac{\sum_{i=1}^r \sigma_i^2}{\sum_{i=1}^{N_s} \sigma_i^2} \ge 1 - \epsilon_{\text{POD}}

The reduced basis is $\mathbf{U}_r = [\mathbf{u}_1, \dots, \mathbf{u}_r]$.

D.1.4 Galerkin Projection

Approximate the state as $\mathbf{x}(t) \approx \bar{\mathbf{x}} + \mathbf{U}_r \mathbf{a}(t)$, where $\mathbf{a}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^r$ are the modal coefficients. Substituting into the governing ODE and projecting onto $\mathbf{U}_r$ yields:

\dot{\mathbf{a}} = \mathbf{U}_r^\top \mathbf{F}(\bar{\mathbf{x}} + \mathbf{U}_r \mathbf{a}, t; \boldsymbol{\mu}) \equiv \mathbf{f}_r(\mathbf{a}, t; \boldsymbol{\mu})

D.2 Discrete Empirical Interpolation Method (DEIM)

For nonlinear terms, evaluating $\mathbf{F}$ at the full state requires $O(N)$ operations, negating the computational savings of the reduced model. DEIM [A13] approximates a nonlinear function $\mathbf{g}(\mathbf{x})$ as:

\mathbf{g}(\mathbf{x}) \approx \mathbf{W} \mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x})

where $\mathbf{W}$ is a POD basis for the nonlinear snapshots and $\mathbf{c}(\mathbf{x})$ is chosen to interpolate $\mathbf{g}$ at a set of $m$ interpolation points.

DEIM Algorithm:

1. Collect snapshots of the nonlinear term $\mathbf{g}_k = \mathbf{g}(\mathbf{x}_k)$.
2. Compute POD basis $\mathbf{W} = [\mathbf{w}_1, \dots, \mathbf{w}_m]$ for $\{\mathbf{g}_k\}$.
3. Select interpolation indices $\mathcal{P} = [p_1, \dots, p_m]$ greedily:
   · $p_1 = \arg\max |\mathbf{w}_1|$
   · For $j = 2, \dots, m$:
     · Solve $(\mathbf{P}_{j-1}^\top \mathbf{W}_{j-1}) \mathbf{c} = \mathbf{P}_{j-1}^\top \mathbf{w}_j$
     · Compute residual $\mathbf{r} = \mathbf{w}_j - \mathbf{W}_{j-1} \mathbf{c}$
     · $p_j = \arg\max |\mathbf{r}|$
4. The DEIM approximation is $\mathbf{g} \approx \mathbf{W} (\mathbf{P}^\top \mathbf{W})^{-1} \mathbf{P}^\top \mathbf{g}$, where $\mathbf{P} = [\mathbf{e}_{p_1}, \dots, \mathbf{e}_{p_m}]$.

Evaluating the DEIM approximation requires only $m$ evaluations of $\mathbf{g}$ (at the interpolation points) rather than $N$.

D.3 Error Analysis

Theorem D.1 (POD Error Bound). Let $\mathbf{X}$ be the snapshot matrix and $\mathbf{U}_r$ the rank-$r$ POD basis. For any state $\mathbf{x}$ in the span of the snapshots,

\|\mathbf{x} - \mathbf{U}_r \mathbf{U}_r^\top \mathbf{x}\|_2 \le \sqrt{\sum_{i=r+1}^{N_s} \sigma_i^2}

Proof. This is a standard result of the Eckart-Young-Mirsky theorem for low-rank approximation. ∎

Theorem D.2 (DEIM Error Bound). Let $\mathbf{g}$ be the nonlinear function and $\tilde{\mathbf{g}}$ its DEIM approximation. Then

\|\mathbf{g} - \tilde{\mathbf{g}}\|_2 \le \|(\mathbf{P}^\top \mathbf{W})^{-1}\|_2 \cdot \|\mathbf{g} - \mathbf{W} \mathbf{W}^\top \mathbf{g}\|_2

The factor $\|(\mathbf{P}^\top \mathbf{W})^{-1}\|_2$ is bounded by a quantity depending on the DEIM interpolation points [A13].

