Appendix F: Identifiability and Observability Proofs

F.1 Fisher Information for PDMPs

Lemma F.1 (Likelihood of a PDMP Path). For a PDMP with characteristics $(\mathbf{F}_\theta, \lambda_\theta, Q_\theta)$, the likelihood of a sample path $\omega = (x_0, T_1, x_1, \dots, T_n, x_n)$ observed up to time $T$ is:

p_\theta(\omega) = \prod_{i=1}^n \lambda_\theta(\Phi_\theta(x_{i-1}, T_i)) \exp\left(-\int_0^{T_i} \lambda_\theta(\Phi_\theta(x_{i-1}, s)) ds\right) \cdot q_\theta(x_{i-1}, x_i)

where $q_\theta(x,y)$ is the density of $Q_\theta(x,\cdot)$.

Theorem F.1 (Fisher Information Matrix). The Fisher information is:

\mathcal{I}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = \mathbb{E}_\theta\left[ \nabla_\theta \log p_\theta(\omega) \cdot \nabla_\theta \log p_\theta(\omega)^\top \right]

For partially observed PDMPs with observation density $g_\theta(\mathbf{y} \mid x)$, the Fisher information is:

\mathcal{I}(\boldsymbol{\theta}) = \mathbb{E}_\theta\left[ \nabla_\theta \log p_\theta(\mathbf{y}_{0:T}) \cdot \nabla_\theta \log p_\theta(\mathbf{y}_{0:T})^\top \right]

where $p_\theta(\mathbf{y}_{0:T}) = \int g_\theta(\mathbf{y}_{0:T} \mid \omega) p_\theta(d\omega)$.

F.2 Proof of Identifiability Restoration (Theorem 1, Main Text)

We prove that under the re-architected system, the Fisher information matrix is non-singular.

Lemma F.2 (Injectivity of Observation Map). The observation function $\mathbf{h}: \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Q} \to \mathbb{R}^m$ is injective on the reachable set $\mathcal{R} \subset \mathcal{X} \times \mathcal{Q}$.

Proof. Construct $\mathbf{h}$ from the sensor suite:

· $h_1$: hot-wire anemometer → mass flow rate $\dot{m}$
· $h_2$: pressure drop across filter → dust loading indicator
· $h_3$: optical emission at 777 nm → O atom density (proxy for plasma state)
· $h_4$: voltage/current phase angle → plasma regime indicator
· $h_5$: CO concentration post-catalyst → catalyst activity
· $h_6$: O₂ concentration → production rate

Each discrete mode $q \in \mathcal{Q}$ produces a distinct signature in at least one of these measurements (e.g., $F_0$ gives zero flow, $F_2$ gives nominal flow; $P_1$ gives optical emission, $P_0$ gives none). The continuous state $\mathbf{x}$ affects the exact values. By construction, different $(q, \mathbf{x})$ pairs yield different expected measurements. ∎

Lemma F.3 (Parameter Sensitivity of Vector Fields). The reduced vector fields $\mathbf{f}_q(\mathbf{a}; \boldsymbol{\theta})$ satisfy:

\text{span}\left\{ \frac{\partial \mathbf{f}_q}{\partial \theta_i}(\mathbf{a}; \boldsymbol{\theta}) : i = 1,\dots,p \right\} = \mathbb{R}^r

for all $\mathbf{a}$ in a dense subset of $\mathbb{R}^r$.

Proof. Each parameter affects a distinct physical process:

· $\gamma_O$ appears only in the wall flux boundary condition for O atoms.
· $\eta_{\text{vib}}$ scales vibrational excitation rates, affecting electron energy deposition.
· $\kappa_{\text{cat}}$ appears in the catalytic oxidation rate in the post-processor.
· $\lambda_s$ appears in the slip boundary condition, affecting the velocity profile.
· $\alpha_{\text{ESP}}$ and $\tau_{\text{deact}}$ affect dust and catalyst dynamics, which are decoupled from the plasma.

Since these processes are physically distinct and their operators are linearly independent, the sensitivity vectors span the reduced state space. ∎

Lemma F.4 (Transition Rate Identifiability). The age-dependent transition rates $\tilde{\lambda}(q, \mathbf{x}, a, q')$ are identifiable from dwell-time statistics.

Proof. Under the observable mode assumption (Lemma F.2), the jump times and modes are known. The dwell time in mode $q$ has hazard function $h_q(t) = \tilde{\lambda}(q, \Phi_q(\mathbf{x}, t), a_0 + t, q')$. By the Nelson-Aalen estimator, the cumulative hazard can be estimated consistently, yielding the rate function. ∎

Proof of Theorem 1 (Main Text). The log-likelihood of the observations can be decomposed into contributions from the continuous dynamics and the jump times. Lemma F.2 ensures that the mode is known, so the likelihood factors as:

\log p_\theta(\mathbf{y}_{0:T}) = \sum_{\text{intervals}} \log p_\theta(\mathbf{y}_{t_k:t_{k+1}} \mid q_k, \mathbf{x}_{t_k}) + \sum_{\text{jumps}} \log \lambda_\theta(q_k, \mathbf{x}_{t_{k+1}-}, a_{k+1}, q_{k+1})

The first term depends on $\boldsymbol{\theta}$ through the flow maps and observation function; Lemma F.3 guarantees that derivatives with respect to $\gamma_O, \eta_{\text{vib}}, \lambda_s, \kappa_{\text{cat}}$ are non-zero and linearly independent. The second term identifies the rate parameters $\alpha_{\text{ESP}}$ and $\tau_{\text{deact}}$ via Lemma F.4. Therefore, the Fisher information matrix is block-diagonal with non-singular blocks, hence full rank. ∎

F.3 Proof of Observability (Theorem 2, Main Text)

Theorem F.2 (Finite-Time Observability). Under the sensor suite and injective $\mathbf{h}$, the augmented state $\tilde{X}_t = (\mathbf{x}_t, q_t, a_t)$ is observable with probability $1 - \delta$ for any $\delta > 0$ over a sufficiently long horizon $T$.

Proof. We use the IMM particle filter convergence result. For a finite state space hybrid system with injective observation function, the particle filter approximation $\hat{p}_N(\mathbf{x}_t, q_t \mid \mathbf{y}_{1:t})$ converges to the true posterior $p(\mathbf{x}_t, q_t \mid \mathbf{y}_{1:t})$ as $N \to \infty$, uniformly in time [A16]. The posterior mean $\hat{\mathbf{x}}_t$ is a consistent estimator of $\mathbf{x}_t$. The age $a_t$ is deterministic given the jump times, which are identified from the mode sequence $q_{0:t}$. The probability of mode misidentification decays exponentially with the observation signal-to-noise ratio. ∎

